### One-Way Push from Server to Client

SSE is simpler than WebSockets when you only need the server to push updates to the client (not the other way around).

```
WebSocket: bidirectional (client ↔ server)
SSE:       server → client only
```

#### Server

```js
app.get('/api/notifications/stream', authenticate, (req, res) => {
  // Set SSE headers
  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/event-stream');
  res.setHeader('Cache-Control', 'no-cache');
  res.setHeader('Connection', 'keep-alive');
  res.flushHeaders(); // Send headers immediately

  // Send a ping every 30s to keep connection alive
  const keepAlive = setInterval(() => {
    res.write(':ping\n\n');
  }, 30000);

  // Subscribe to user-specific events
  const unsubscribe = pubsub.subscribe(
    `user:${req.user.id}:notifications`,
    (notification) => {
      res.write(`data: ${JSON.stringify(notification)}\n\n`);
    }
  );

  // Clean up when client disconnects
  req.on('close', () => {
    clearInterval(keepAlive);
    unsubscribe();
  });
});
```

#### Client

```js
const eventSource = new EventSource('/api/notifications/stream');

eventSource.onmessage = (event) => {
  const notification = JSON.parse(event.data);
  showNotification(notification);
};

eventSource.onerror = () => {
  // Browser auto-reconnects after a delay
  console.log('Reconnecting...');
};
```

#### SSE vs WebSockets: When to Use Which

| | SSE | WebSocket |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Direction** | Server → Client | Bidirectional |
| **Protocol** | HTTP | Custom (ws://) |
| **Auto-reconnect** | Built-in | Manual |
| **Best for** | Notifications, live feeds | Chat, games, collaboration |

